{
  "dom_id": "step-q80843",
  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{x\\in N:x<5\\}$ and $B=\\{x\\in N:2x\\ge 4\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $B⊆A$\n\nIII. $∅⊆B$\n\nIV. $B⊇A$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers less than $5.$ So,\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $2x\\ge 4,$ that is to say $x\\ge 2.$ So,\n\n$$\nB=\\{2,3,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $1\\in A$ but $1\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $5\\in B$ but $5\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅=\\{\\}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
